<p>A business website usually takes anywhere from a few weeks to several months to build. <a href="https://www.godaddy.com/resources/uk/smallbusiness/how-long-does-it-take-to-build-a-website" rel="noopener" target="_blank">GoDaddy's small business guide</a> puts a simple site built with a web designer at "a few weeks" and a large, complex custom site at "several months." Where your project lands depends on how many pages you need, how custom the design is, and how quickly the content and decisions come together.</p>

<p>Here's the part most people don't expect: the code is rarely what takes longest. Strategy, writing, photos and feedback rounds are where timelines stretch or shrink. Below we'll walk through each stage, what affects it, and what you can do to keep your launch on track.</p>

<h2>What decides how long a website takes?</h2>

<p>Every project is different, but the same handful of things move the timeline almost every time:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Number of pages.</strong> A five page site for a local service business is a very different job from a site with dozens of service, location and product pages.</li>
  <li><strong>How custom it is.</strong> A template with your logo dropped in is quick. A design built from scratch around your brand takes more planning, design and testing.</li>
  <li><strong>Features.</strong> Booking, online ordering, member areas, custom forms, animation and integrations with other software each add design and testing time.</li>
  <li><strong>Content readiness.</strong> If your copy, photos and logo files are ready, the build moves. If they're not, everything waits on them.</li>
  <li><strong>Feedback speed.</strong> One decision maker who replies within a couple of days keeps things moving. Feedback that has to go through a committee slows every round.</li>
  <li><strong>Your brand.</strong> If your logo or colors need work first, that comes before the website. Our post on <a href="/blog/rebrand-or-refresh-logo-albuquerque">whether to rebrand or refresh</a> can help you decide.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The stages of building a website</h2>

<p>Most professional website projects follow a similar path. Here's how ours works, and what happens in each stage so you know where the time goes.</p>

<h3>1. Discovery and strategy</h3>

<p>Every DYAD project starts with a BRAND$TORM™ consultation, a collaborative discovery session about your purpose, long term vision and marketing strategy. From there we put together a scope of work, recommendations, a forecasted timeline and an investment schedule.</p>

<p>This stage is short compared to the rest, but it saves the most time. A clear scope means fewer surprises, fewer "can we also add..." moments halfway through, and a timeline built around your actual project instead of a guess.</p>

<h3>2. Content: copy and photos</h3>

<p>This is the stage that most often turns a few weeks into a few months. Every page needs words that answer your customers' questions and are written for how people search, plus real photos of your team, your place and your work.</p>

<p>If you're writing the copy yourself, block out real time on your calendar for it. If you need photos, schedule the shoot early, since weather, staff schedules and seasonal timing can all push it back.</p>

<h3>3. Design</h3>

<p>Design usually starts with the homepage and one key inside page, then rolls out to the rest of the site once the look is approved. Expect at least one round of feedback here. The clearer your notes, the faster the next round goes.</p>

<h3>4. Development and testing</h3>

<p>Now the approved design gets built. A good build includes testing on phones, tablets and desktops, checking every form, and setting up the search basics like page titles, descriptions and a sitemap. Mobile testing deserves real time, since plenty of your customers will see the site on a phone first.</p>

<h3>5. Review, launch and the first few weeks</h3>

<p>Before launch you'll review the finished site and approve final changes. On launch day the site goes live, analytics get checked, and links from your Google Business Profile and social profiles get updated. If you're replacing an old site, redirects from old page addresses matter here too. Our <a href="/blog/website-redesign-checklist-new-mexico">website redesign checklist</a> covers that step in detail.</p>

<p>One timing note people often miss: going live isn't the same as showing up on Google. <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/ask-google-to-recrawl" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google's own documentation</a> says "Crawling can take anywhere from a few days to a few weeks." Submitting your sitemap in Google Search Console helps, but build that wait into your plans if you're launching ahead of a busy season or an event.</p>

<h2>How to keep your website project on schedule</h2>

<p>You can't control everything, but most delays come from a short list of things that are easy to get ahead of:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Gather your logins before kickoff.</strong> Domain registrar, hosting, Google Business Profile, Google Analytics and Search Console. Hunting down an old password from a former employee can stall a launch for days.</li>
  <li><strong>Have your logo files ready.</strong> Vector files (like .svg, .ai or .eps) and a list of your brand colors and fonts. If you have a <a href="/blog/brand-style-guide-small-business">brand style guide</a>, share it on day one.</li>
  <li><strong>Pick one decision maker.</strong> Others can weigh in, but one person should gather the feedback and give the final yes.</li>
  <li><strong>Write down the questions customers ask most.</strong> They're the backbone of good website copy and save a lot of back and forth.</li>
  <li><strong>Agree on feedback windows.</strong> Decide up front how quickly you'll review each round, and protect that time.</li>
  <li><strong>Separate "launch" from "later."</strong> Decide which pages and features you need on day one and which can come after.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Launch the core, then keep building</h2>

<p>That last point is worth a closer look. A website doesn't have to be finished to be useful. Launching the pages that matter most first, then adding to the site over time, gets you in front of customers sooner and lets real visitors show you what to build next.</p>

<p>Our work with <a href="/work/lumanta">Lumanta</a> followed that kind of path. It started with a BRAND$TORM, then a logo refresh, then a fully custom website. After launch, we added three new pages, Catch, Shift and Rise, each written to answer a real question people search, like how to let go of negative thoughts.</p>

<h2>Is a faster website always better?</h2>

<p>A site you can launch this weekend with a website builder is a fine option if you need a basic online presence quickly. Just know what you're trading. Templates tend to look like everyone else's, and a rushed site often skips the strategy, copy and testing that help it get found and turn visitors into calls.</p>

<p>The better question isn't "how fast?" but "what does this site need to do, and what's a realistic timeline to do it well?" If a designer can't answer that clearly before you start, that's worth noticing.</p>

<h2>Getting a timeline for your website</h2>

<p>The only way to get a real number for your project is to talk through your goals, your pages and your content. That's exactly what our <a href="/web-design">custom website design and development</a> process is built for: a BRAND$TORM™ session first, then a scope of work with a forecasted timeline before any building begins. If you're planning a new site in Albuquerque or anywhere in New Mexico, <a href="/#contact">book a call</a> and let's map out what yours will take.</p>
